From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yewsifofficial <yewsifofficial@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:27:24 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] nifm: log the address the game is actually given, and find
 one when the route has none
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

DM_IMPORTS=1 answered which overload this game uses, and it is the opposite
of what the last patch assumed: GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddress(in_addr*) is
bound and nn::socket::InAddr is "not referenced by this game". The bound one
is the only one of the pair that never logged, which is why no nifm line has
appeared in any log while the probes carried 0.0.0.0. It logs now.

That address matters more than the probes suggested. Nextendo's responder
reads only the first four bytes of a probe, the test id, and ignores the
rest, so 0.0.0.0 there costs nothing — and indeed the NAT check succeeds.
The station URLs are where it counts: their server pairs a private candidate
with a public one and refuses to bridge a host that reports no private
address (natbridge.go selectStations with isPrivateIP, RFC1918 and CGNAT
only), and 0.0.0.0 is neither. Their note on that path describes what we
see: the console "stops after MatchMakingExt m=1 and re-registers in a loop
until the game shows a connection error", and our last call before the error
is match making (ext) 0x32 method 1.

So nx_local_ip no longer gives up when the default route yields nothing: it
falls back to the first address of an interface that is up and is not the
loopback, Wi-Fi ahead of the rest, and says so. A UDP bind now logs its
address as well as its port, which shows the same thing from the game's
side: a game that knows its address binds to it.

Upstream-Status: fork-only (online play is out of nxnative's scope)
Reason: Nextendo online play for Super Smash Bros. Ultimate
Issue: yewsiff/NX2Apple (no tracker: issues are disabled on this fork)

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017xdHQoEipBX2wTqN8BNnMv
---
 dm/host/hle_net.c            |  4 +++-
 dm/host/hle_offline.c        |  7 ++++++-
 dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m | 37 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 docs/nextendo.md             |  2 +-
 4 files changed, 40 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dm/host/hle_net.c b/dm/host/hle_net.c
index ad3c0bc..aea7ec9 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_net.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_net.c
@@ -467,7 +467,9 @@ static int on_bind(int fd, const void *a, unsigned n) {
         in.sin_addr.s_addr = 0;
         r = bind(h, (struct sockaddr *)&in, sizeof in);
     }
-    if (type == SK_SOCK_DGRAM) NXLOG("socket %d: bound to UDP port %u%s%s", fd, ntohs(in.sin_port), r ? ", failed: " : "", r ? strerror(errno) : "");
+    // The address as well as the port: a game that knows its own address binds to it, and one that does not binds to 0.0.0.0 — the same address
+    // it would then put in the private half of its station URL, which a server pairing a private candidate with a public one will not accept
+    if (type == SK_SOCK_DGRAM) { char d[24]; NXLOG("socket %d: bound to UDP %s%s%s", fd, ip_str(in.sin_addr.s_addr, in.sin_port, d), r ? ", failed: " : "", r ? strerror(errno) : ""); }
     else BTRACE(fd, "socket %d: bind %s -> %d%s%s", fd, ip_str(in.sin_addr.s_addr, in.sin_port, d), r, r ? ", errno " : "", r ? strerror(errno) : "");
     if (r) return on_err(errno);
     pthread_mutex_lock(&g_efd_m);
diff --git a/dm/host/hle_offline.c b/dm/host/hle_offline.c
index 0d89c63..597faf2 100644
--- a/dm/host/hle_offline.c
+++ b/dm/host/hle_offline.c
@@ -166,9 +166,14 @@ HLE_FORCE("_ZN2nn4nifm22GetCurrentIpConfigInfoEPNS_6socket6InAddrES3_S3_S3_S3_",
 // calls whichever its SDK has. Super Smash Bros. Ultimate's is the InAddr one, which went unhooked, so the game ran the SDK's own: that asks the
 // nifm service over IPC, which is not answered here, and the game went online believing its address to be 0.0.0.0. Its NAT check carried that
 // address, and Pia, with no address of its own to put in a station URL, gave up before it ever reached the other player
-HLE_FORCE("_ZN2nn4nifm26GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddressEP7in_addr", "offline: no IP (SDK 10 overload; online: the host's)", Result, h_nifm_GetIp10, uint32_t *a) {
+// This is the overload Super Smash Bros. Ultimate references (DM_IMPORTS=1 shows it bound and the nn::socket::InAddr one unreferenced), and it is
+// the address the game puts in the private half of its station URL. Nextendo's server pairs a private candidate with a public one and refuses to
+// bridge a host that reports no private address at all (natbridge.go selectStations / isPrivateIP, which takes RFC1918 and CGNAT only), so what
+// this returns decides whether a session can be joined. It said nothing until now, which is why no nifm line ever appeared in a log
+HLE_FORCE("_ZN2nn4nifm26GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddressEP7in_addr", "offline: no IP (online: the host's)", Result, h_nifm_GetIp10, uint32_t *a) {
     uint32_t ip = dm_net_online() ? nx_local_ip() : 0;
     if (a) *a = htonl(ip);
+    NXLOG("nifm: the game asks for its own address -> %u.%u.%u.%u%s", ip >> 24, ip >> 16 & 255, ip >> 8 & 255, ip & 255, ip ? "" : " (none: reported as a failure)");
     return ip ? RES_OK : RES_FAIL;
 }
 HLE_FORCE("_ZN2nn4nifm18GetCurrentIpConfigEPNS0_10Ipv4ConfigEPiPNS0_10Ipv6ConfigES3_ii", "offline: no IP configuration (SDK 21 overload)", Result, h_nifm_IpCfg21,
diff --git a/dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m b/dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m
index 39d356e..91fe19d 100644
--- a/dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m
+++ b/dm/host/net_nextendo_apple.m
@@ -13,6 +13,8 @@
 // reads the "nnex" claim. The RSA key is made once and kept in the Keychain with a random device ID.
 #include <TargetConditionals.h>
 #import <Foundation/Foundation.h>
+#include <ifaddrs.h>
+#include <net/if.h>
 #import <Security/Security.h>
 #import <Network/Network.h>
 #import <CommonCrypto/CommonDigest.h>
@@ -172,16 +174,37 @@ int nx_dest_allowed(uint32_t ip, int udp) {
     if (udp) return 1;
     return ip == NX_BRIDGE_IP || ip == C.server || ip == C.nat;
 }
-uint32_t nx_local_ip(void) {   // the address the default route would use: connect a UDP socket (no packet is sent) and read its name
+// The device's own address on the network it plays over. The game puts it in the private half of its station URL, and a server that pairs a
+// private candidate with a public one has nothing to bridge without it, so a zero here is a session that cannot be joined
+uint32_t nx_local_ip(void) {
+    // What the default route would use: connect a UDP socket (which sends nothing) and read its name
     int s = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
-    if (s < 0) return 0;
-    struct sockaddr_in a = {.sin_len = sizeof a, .sin_family = AF_INET, .sin_port = htons(9), .sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(C.server ? C.server : 0x08080808)};
     uint32_t ip = 0;
-    if (!connect(s, (struct sockaddr *)&a, sizeof a)) {
-        struct sockaddr_in me; socklen_t ml = sizeof me;
-        if (!getsockname(s, (struct sockaddr *)&me, &ml)) ip = ntohl(me.sin_addr.s_addr);
+    if (s >= 0) {
+        struct sockaddr_in a = {.sin_len = sizeof a, .sin_family = AF_INET, .sin_port = htons(9), .sin_addr.s_addr = htonl(C.server ? C.server : 0x08080808)};
+        if (!connect(s, (struct sockaddr *)&a, sizeof a)) {
+            struct sockaddr_in me; socklen_t ml = sizeof me;
+            if (!getsockname(s, (struct sockaddr *)&me, &ml)) ip = ntohl(me.sin_addr.s_addr);
+        }
+        close(s);
+    }
+    if (ip) return ip;
+    // No route yet (the interface still coming up, or the address asked for before the network is ready): the first address of an interface that
+    // is up and is not the loopback, Wi-Fi (en*) ahead of the rest
+    struct ifaddrs *list = NULL;
+    if (getifaddrs(&list)) return 0;
+    uint32_t best = 0, any = 0;
+    for (struct ifaddrs *f = list; f; f = f->ifa_next) {
+        if (!f->ifa_addr || f->ifa_addr->sa_family != AF_INET) continue;
+        if (!(f->ifa_flags & IFF_UP) || (f->ifa_flags & IFF_LOOPBACK)) continue;
+        uint32_t v = ntohl(((struct sockaddr_in *)f->ifa_addr)->sin_addr.s_addr);
+        if (!v || (v >> 24) == 127) continue;
+        if (!any) any = v;
+        if (!best && f->ifa_name && f->ifa_name[0] == 'e' && f->ifa_name[1] == 'n') best = v;
     }
-    close(s);
+    freeifaddrs(list);
+    ip = best ? best : any;
+    if (ip) NXLOG("the default route gave no address of ours; an interface that is up has %u.%u.%u.%u", ip >> 24, ip >> 16 & 255, ip >> 8 & 255, ip & 255);
     return ip;
 }
 
diff --git a/docs/nextendo.md b/docs/nextendo.md
index 3527f65..ad7c342 100644
--- a/docs/nextendo.md
+++ b/docs/nextendo.md
@@ -24,7 +24,7 @@ runs offline exactly as before.
 | Account | `net_nextendo_apple.m` | OAuth 2.0 authorization code + PKCE in an in-app sheet (iPhone) or the browser (Mac), redirect caught on `127.0.0.1`; refresh token in the Keychain; `GET /api/nex-token` gives the NEX login token and the PID |
 | ID token | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_account.c` | the network service account's ID token: a JWT shaped like Nintendo BaaS's with the PID as `sub` and the NEX token as `nnex` |
 | Sockets | `dm/host/hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | `nn::socket` on the host's BSD sockets (non-blocking at the host; the game's blocking calls wait in slices). TCP reaches Nextendo only, UDP any address (Pia's peers). Errnos and `O_NONBLOCK` (0x800) are Linux's, as the console's library has them; only the socket options whose number means the same on Horizon and on Apple's kernel reach the host socket, the others are kept and read back as set |
-| nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's. A game calls whichever overload its SDK has, so both are hooked for `GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddress` (`nn::socket::InAddr` in `hle_net.c`, `in_addr` in `hle_offline.c`) and for `GetCurrentIpConfigInfo` — each exactly once, since the loader stops a game whose forced-takeover list holds a name twice |
+| nifm | `hle_net.c`, `hle_offline.c` | the network is available; the IP configuration is the host's, logged when online because the game puts that address in the private half of its station URL and a server pairing a private candidate with a public one has nothing to bridge without it. A game calls whichever overload its SDK has, so both are hooked for `GetCurrentPrimaryIpAddress` (`nn::socket::InAddr` in `hle_net.c`, `in_addr` in `hle_offline.c`) and for `GetCurrentIpConfigInfo` — each exactly once, since the loader stops a game whose forced-takeover list holds a name twice |
 | NEX login | `dm/games/01006A800016E000/patches.py`, `hle_net.c` | gate `OBS_NEX_LOGIN` on the login job gives NEX's NgsFacade a direct server address, `10.13.37.1:443`, where NEX speaks its WebSocket without TLS |
 | nn::ssl | `hle_net.c` | a connection's handshake on a socket connected to the game server swaps the bridge in for that socket, with the host name the game set; Read, Write, Peek and Poll are then the socket's. This is how Mario Kart 8 Deluxe's NEX client logs in, with no patch of the game |
 | TLS bridge | `net_nextendo_apple.m`, `hle_net.c` | a TCP connection to `10.13.37.1`, or to the game server on the port its station URL names, is a local socket whose other end goes to the server in TLS (Network.framework), the server's key checked against the pins of `DM_NEXTENDO_PIN`, the `Host` header rewritten. Port 443 on the server is the game's own HTTPS and is left plain: `nn::ssl`'s handshake puts the bridge in its place with the name the game set, and if the game writes in clear there instead, its first bytes bring the bridge in |
